      "name": "Are faux wood shutters waterproof?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "They're highly moisture-resistant rather than fully waterproof. This makes them well-suited for kitchens and bathrooms — steam and splashes won't cause damage — but they're not designed for outdoor use or direct water contact."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do faux wood shutters look cheap?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Not if you buy from a reputable supplier. Poor-quality faux wood can look plasticky, but well-made PVC shutters with a good paint finish are genuinely difficult to distinguish from real wood once fitted. The key is the quality of the finish and the precision of the manufacturing."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can faux wood shutters be painted?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Most faux wood shutters come pre-finished in a range of colours and aren't designed to be repainted at home. Unlike timber, the surface doesn't take paint in the same way. If you want a specific colour, it's worth choosing it at the order stage rather than trying to paint them later."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Are faux wood shutters worth it?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "For most homeowners, yes. They offer the look of a premium window dressing at a more accessible price point, they're built to last, and they require very little upkeep. If you're comparing them to replacing cheap blinds every few years, the economics stack up clearly in favour of shutters over the long term."
      }
    }
